is that some of these small  business ideas start as a hobby mm-hmm  how do you tell them to know  when when they're moving from a hobby  into a successful business if they  should or how they're doing it or at  what point we quit calling it aa B and  we need to just say what it is it's now  a business and so on I think first you  really have to ask yourself is this  something that I want to treat like a  business and that means that you're  going to treat it for the purpose of  making a profit now you're still serving  people but the difference between a  hobby and a business is that a hobby  costs you money and a business makes you  money and it seems really simple but a  lot of people not just women have  hobbies that they say they want to turn  into businesses but they keep treating  them like hobbies and they don't look at  their profit margin or any of their  financials and they're like well I just  love it that's all that matters and if  you want to turn this into a business  that's great but you've got to start  treating it like a business so the first  question would be to ask yourself do I  want to do that do I want to take this  thing that I love that I do right now  for fun as an outlet and have the  responsibility of treating it like a  business because if you want the  business to be successful you have to  treat it differently than you do as a  creative outlet so if you want to do  that then we can give you the steps to  do that and if you don't that's fine and  by the way you can make a profit I still  be creative right absolutely you can  still enjoy it so many people say well I  enjoy it so I felt guilty making money  as if for some reason you're not  supposed to enjoy the things that you do  for an income but but you can do that  you can enjoy it you can have fun many  of the women I talked to they say this  business and they're treating it like a  business they're successful with their  side business they say it is this outlet  for me that is one of the areas of my  life that I give to and it actually  gives back to me it actually gives me  energy versus depleting my energy so you  definitely can build a business that  still does those things for you but a  business will have additional  responsibilities of plans and financials  and record-keeping and and people that  you're accountable to whereas with a  hobby you don't have those things so you  really need to ask yourself if you're  ready to do that before you turn that  into a business I find myself in the  entree leadership material working with  men or women often almost reprimanding  someone who thinks they're running a  business but it's yet to make a profit  and always tell them this is a hobby  right and so it's kind of the other way  and you're in you're on one side of this  where are you the other side of this in  a sense where you're saying okay this is  a hobby let me show you how to make it a  business right rather than it's a  business is not cutting it right and if  you have a  be that you enjoy and you are able to  turn it into a business that you are  good with the amount of responsibility  how great is that because you're  combining something you love with making  money so you enjoy it and you're having  good time plus you're contributing to  your family income so it can be done and  it can be very very enjoyable but  there's a process you go through you  can't like you said you can't keep  treating like a hobby and not making  money because first of all your family  is going to be funding it so you're it  has serious implications to your family  budget  additionally especially for moms that  have all these responsibilities to the  household and family it's very difficult  to justify 20 or 30 or 40 hours a week  away from your family just because you  love it you know you need to have a  financial roi to say I'm spending this  time painting doing crafts teaching  lessons tutoring and I'm also have this  financial ROI to show for it  yeah 